<h2>What it means</h2>

<div class="finds">
  <div class="find">
    <h3>vsim is already shaped like <code>-M none</code></h3>
    <p>Both put the CPU behind a SystemC bus with the devices hanging off it. vsim reaches its bus through <code>axi_to_tlm_bridge</code> from AXI pins; FastSim's CPU reaches the router through <code>QemuInitiatorSocket</code>. Different front end, same shape &mdash; which is why the two platforms can become one.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="find warn">
    <h3>Machine mode has nothing to share</h3>
    <p>Nine modules, one real device, and four of the nine exist only to stop the SystemC kernel exiting because it has no work. Every device the guest actually uses is inside QEMU, with no module on the bus. There is also no <code>QemuCpu</code>, so no <code>halt</code>, no <code>reset</code>, and no quantum keeper &mdash; the three handles a handoff needs.</p>
    <p>This mode is the right tool for its own job: run an unmodified QEMU board and expose one window to a SystemC model. It is the wrong tool for one shared platform.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="find">
    <h3>The missing pieces are the same three either way</h3>
    <p><code>-M none</code> already covers memory, both UARTs, the SMU, the interrupt controller, the timer, the small APB blocks, the loader and the exit path. What no FastSim mode has is <strong>SimControl</strong> (the syscall window, where the guest fd bug lives) and a <strong>debug module</strong> &mdash; and vsim has both. What no vsim build has is <strong>PLIC and PLMT</strong>.</p>
  </div>
